# Test Isolation and State Rules

## Purpose

These rules define how tests must manage state and avoid cross-test dependencies.

---

## Rules

- Tests must be independent.
- Tests must not depend on execution order.
- Tests must not depend on state created by another test.
- Tests must be safe for parallel execution unless explicitly marked otherwise.
- Shared state must be read-only or safely isolated.
- Data created by a test must be unique, isolated, or cleaned up.
- Cleanup must be safe to retry.
- Do not use global mutable state to coordinate tests.
- Do not rely on previous browser/session state unless provided through an approved fixture or storage state.

---

## Setup

Use setup through:

- fixtures;
- builders;
- API setup;
- seeded datasets;
- storage state;
- project-approved test data providers.

Do not perform hidden setup in unrelated helpers.

---

## Cleanup

Cleanup is required when created data can affect other tests.

Cleanup may be done through:

- API cleanup;
- fixture teardown;
- database cleanup only if project-approved;
- environment reset mechanisms.

---

## Main Principle

A test should pass or fail based on its own setup and assertions, not on previous tests.
